\label{def:mGA}
Let $S$ be either a finite abelian group or a power of a finite subset of a field. Let $A$ be an $\ell\times k$ integer matrix. We set
\begin{align}\label{eq:mGAdef}
m_S(A) := \max_{\stackrel{W \subseteq [k]}{|W| \geq 2}} \frac{|W|-1}{|W|-1+\mathrm{rank}_S(A_{\overline{W}}) - \mathrm{rank}_S(A)}.
\end{align}
Here we recall that $\mathrm{rank}_S(A_{\overline{W}}):=0$ if 
$\overline{W}=\emptyset$.
We call $A$ \emph{strictly balanced with respect to $S$} if the expression in (\ref{eq:mGAdef}) is  maximised precisely when $W=[k]$.

\begin{align}\label{eq:mGAdef}
m_S(A) := \max_{\stackrel{W \subseteq [k]}{|W| \geq 2}} \frac{|W|-1}{|W|-1+\mathrm{rank}_S(A_{\overline{W}}) - \mathrm{rank}_S(A)}.
\end{align}

\label{rmk:m(a)abb}
Observe that $m_S(A)$ is not always well-defined since the denominator in~\eqref{eq:mGAdef} might be zero. However, if $(A,S)$ is abundant then for all $|W| \geq 2$ we have 
\begin{align}\label{eq:rankW2}
\mathrm{rank}_S(A_{\overline{W}}) \geq \mathrm{rank}_S(A) - |W| +2,
\end{align} 
since the rank of a matrix can decrease by at most one by deleting a column.\footnote{This fact 
follows immediately from the definition of $\mathrm{rank}_S(A)$ when
$S$ is the power of a subset of a field $F$. When $S$ is a finite abelian group, it can be proved using equation~\eqref{eq:rank1}.} If~(\ref{eq:rankW2}) is satisfied then the denominator in~\eqref{eq:mGAdef} is always strictly positive and so $m_S(A)$ is well-defined in this case.

\begin{align}\label{eq:rankW2}
\mathrm{rank}_S(A_{\overline{W}}) \geq \mathrm{rank}_S(A) - |W| +2,
\end{align}

\label{examp:mZ4}
Let $A=(2\;\; 2\; -2)$. The number of solutions $x=(x_1,x_2,x_3)$ to $Ax=0$ in $\mathbb Z_4$ is precisely $2\cdot4^2=4^{3-1/2}$: we have $4^2$ choices for $x_1,x_2$ and, for each  fixed choice, we have $2$ choices for $x_3$. It follows from (\ref{eq:rank1}) that $\mathrm{rank}_{\mathbb Z_4}(A)=1/2$. Now, let $W\subseteq[3]$. If $|W|=3$ we trivially have $\mathrm{rank}_{\mathbb Z_4}(A_{\overline W})=0$. If $|W|=2$ then $A_{\overline W}x=0$ is the same as $2x=0$, which has $2=4^{1-1/2}$ solutions in $\mathbb Z_4$. So we have $\mathrm{rank}_{\mathbb Z_4}(A_{\overline W})=1/2$ again. Thus,
$$m_{\mathbb{Z}_4}(A)=\max\left\{\frac{3-1}{3-1+0-1/2},\frac{2-1}{2-1+1/2-1/2}\right\}=4/3.$$

